;; WF2 negative, write channel 3 of 3: a callee's own DECLARED frame.
;;
;; `outer!` hands its parameter `a` to `inner!`, whose CHECKED frame says that
;; slot is written.  `outer!` declares the empty frame, so `a` is written and
;; the frame does not cover it.
;;
;; This is the channel that needs WF1 to exist at all.  `inner!`'s parameter is
;; NOT `^mut`, so channel 2 has nothing to say about this call, and with no
;; declared frame the only sound answer would be "cannot vouch" -- no error,
;; no verification, nothing.  The declaration is what turns an unverifiable
;; call into a decidable one, which is the whole reason WF2/WF3/WF4 all wait
;; on WF1.
;;
;; `inner!` declaring more than it writes is fine and is not what is under
;; test: a frame is an UPPER BOUND, so a body that writes less than it declared
;; honors it.  What matters here is that the bound is what callers must respect.
;;
;; Note the blame lands on the CALLER.  `inner!` is impeccable -- it does no
;; more than it said -- and reporting it would be blaming a function for its
;; caller's understated frame.
;;
;; `inner!` is defined AFTER `outer!` on purpose: the check is deferred until
;; after elaboration precisely so definition order cannot change the answer.
(defstruct Ctr [n : int])

(defn outer! [a : Ctr] #writes [] : int
  (inner! a))

(defn inner! [b : Ctr] #writes [b] : int
  0)

(defn main [] : int 0)
